course: dictionary
lesson: 6846
title: Outrun: faster, further, beyond
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Outrun is a verb. It means to run faster or further than someone or something, so you get away or win. Its past form is outran.
explain: «The dad lets go of the lead: the cows were after the dog, the dog easily outruns them, and the family walks calmly to the gate.» The cows chase the dog, but the dog runs faster than they can, so it gets away. The calm walk to the gate shows the dog is safe.
explain: Outrun can also mean to go beyond something. «Our spending outran our money» means we spent more than we had. To check your meaning, swap in a simpler phrase, such as went beyond, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Outrun means to run faster or further than someone, so you get away or win.
fact: The past form of outrun is outran.
fact: In sport, «outrun the defenders» means to run past them faster than they can follow.
fact: You cannot outrun a wave or a fire, so get out of the way early.
fact: A cheetah is the fastest animal over a short sprint, but a gazelle can outrun a lion over a long distance.
exercise: «The dog easily outruns them.» What does outruns mean? | Runs in a circle around them | Stops and waits for them | Eats their food | *Runs faster than them, so it gets away
exercise: «Our spending outran our money.» What does outran mean here? | Found more money | Saved it all | *Went beyond what we had | Ran away from home
exercise: What should you do about a fire? | Hide under the table | Stay where you are and watch | *Get out of the way early, because you cannot outrun it | Run as fast as you can and hope to beat it
test: Which animal can outrun a lion over a long distance? | Cheetah | *Gazelle | Lion itself | Dog
test: In sport, what does «outrun the defenders» mean? | Stand still and wait | Argue with the referee | *Run past them faster than they can follow | Fall over
exam: Which sentence uses outrun to mean go beyond something? | «The dog outruns the cows.» | «The cheetah sprints.» | *«Our spending outran our money.» | «The family walks to the gate.»
